  "worked_solution": "Recall that if  $A=U\u03a3{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\u03a3$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The matrix $\u03a3$ must be diagonal but not necessarily orthogonal.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix,the matrix $U$ in the singular value decomposition $U\u03a3{V}^{T}$ must be orthogonal $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $V$ must be orthogonal but not necessarily symmetric.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
